{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00488 - MONDO_18172",
  "verbalization": "Altretamine, a chemotherapy agent, is implicated in the treatment of various ovarian tumors through a shared pathway involving cisplatin. The evidence suggests that Altretamine may be repurposed for the treatment of Malignant Sex Cord Stromal Tumor Of Ovary, as it is indicated for similar tumors such as Malignant Dysgerminomatous Germ Cell Tumor Of Ovary and Malignant Sertoli-leydig Cell Tumor Of Ovary, all of which are associated with cisplatin. The mechanism of action likely involves the inhibition of tumor cell proliferation, as cisplatin is known to induce DNA damage and cell death in cancer cells. The presence of a common downstream pathway with cisplatin implies that Altretamine may exert a similar effect, potentially through a shared biological mechanism. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between Altretamine and the target tumor, though the exact mode of action remains to be fully elucidated. The observed scores suggest a plausible relationship, but further experimental validation is needed to confirm the therapeutic potential.",
